            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 1)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "5627dc6e-c943-439e-ab4a-4531b7f24264"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Diocletian</strong><br>\nBorn to a family of low status in Dalmatia, Diocletian rose through the ranks of the military to become a Roman cavalry commander. His early life gave little indication of his future importance, with no noble lineage or wealth to his name. It was his military skill and leadership that paved his path to power.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Rise to Power</strong><br>\nDiocletian's ascension to Roman Emperor in 284 AD was marked by the chaos of the Roman world, where emperors were often violently overthrown. After the murder of Emperor Numerian, Diocletian was proclaimed emperor by the army. He later defeated Numerian's brother Carinus, securing his position as the sole ruler of the Roman Empire.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Governing the Empire</strong><br>\nUnderstanding the difficulties of governing the vast Roman Empire, Diocletian introduced significant administrative reforms. He divided the empire into two, ruling the Eastern Roman Empire himself and appointing a co-emperor, Maximian, to rule the West. This division aimed to make governance more efficient and secure the empire's borders.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
