      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Tetrarchy System</strong><br>\nDiocletian established the Tetrarchy to further stabilize the empire, dividing it into four parts, each ruled by a 'Tetrarch'. Two senior emperors, known as Augusti, and two junior emperors, known as Caesars, shared power under this system. This innovation aimed to prevent civil wars and ease succession crises.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Economic Reforms</strong><br>\nDiocletian enacted economic policies to counteract the empire's financial decay, including issuing a new coin, the 'nummus', to combat inflation. He also set price controls on goods and services to stabilize the economy. These measures, however, met with mixed success and faced opposition and difficulty in enforcement.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Persecution of Christians</strong><br>\nDiocletian is known for the last and greatest persecution of Christians in the Roman Empire. In 303 AD, he ordered Christian buildings to be destroyed and sacred books to be burned. Many Christians were arrested, and some were executed under his edicts, which aimed to restore traditional Roman religious practices.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
