            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Military Campaigns</strong><br>\nThroughout his reign, Diocletian led numerous military campaigns to defend the empire. He fought against Rome's enemies on several fronts, including in Egypt, the Middle East, and along the Danube River. These campaigns were crucial in maintaining the empire's stability and security during his rule.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Abdication and Retirement</strong><br>\nIn a rare move for Roman emperors, Diocletian voluntarily abdicated his throne in 305 AD, citing poor health. He encouraged his co-emperor, Maximian, to do the same, to ensure the smooth succession of their appointed Caesars. Diocletian retired to his palace in Split, Croatia, where he lived out his remaining years.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy of Diocletian</strong><br>\nDiocletian's reign is remembered for bringing stability to an empire in crisis. Despite some of his policies being controversial or unsuccessful, his administrative and military reforms strengthened the Roman Empire. After his retirement, the Tetrarchy eventually collapsed, but the idea of sharing imperial power influenced later political structures.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
